Jahshaka patch 0084 - the voxel occupancy the ambient rides is a property of
the cell, not of the cone.

MEDIA-only patch (one Hlms piece: Samples/Media/Hlms/Pbs/Any/Vct_piece_ps.any).
Studio stages its media from the SOURCE tree (cmake/IncludeOgre.cmake), so every
tree still has to re-run `irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh` to get it - no engine
COMPILE is needed beyond the staging, which is the whole difference from a
SOURCE patch. It OVERLAPS patches 0021, 0033, 0045, 0048, 0066, 0070 and 0083 in
that one file (it amends a sentence of 0021's own comment), so a tree that
already carries those hits `build-ogre.sh`'s "Upstream changed the patched file"
on a re-run: that is the documented per-patch reverse-check blind spot, NOT an
upstream change. The cure is the recorded one - reset the submodule
(`git -C irisgl/thirdparty/ogre-next checkout -- . && git -C
irisgl/thirdparty/ogre-next clean -fd`) and re-run the script.

WHAT THE USER SEES. A surface reads eight to nine display codes BRIGHTER for
standing more than 32 m from the world origin. Nothing about the scene has
changed: move a whole scene AND its camera together by a distance that is an
exact multiple of every cascade's cell, so that not one voxel and not one light
moves inside any volume, and the shaded face of a cube 60 m out is 8-9/255
brighter than the same face at the origin - a STEP at 32 m, flat from there to
180 m. Lane LATTICE-1 measured it (`scripting.e2e.lattice_placement`'s protocol,
2026-09-21) and proved the volumes identical while it happened: voxelsLit
16384/18660/4548/2960 and meanLit to six decimal places, the same at every arm.

WHY. `isNegative` picks which of the two composites packed into an anisotropic
axis texture - the one accumulated towards +axis or the one towards -axis - a
sample reads, from the SIGN of that component of the cone's direction:

    isNegative.x = dirLS.x < 0 ? 0.5f : 0.0f;

For the COLOUR composite that is exactly right, and it is self-healing: the
sample is weighted by `dirLSSquared`, so as a component goes to zero the half it
chose stops mattering. Patch 0021's ESCAPE estimate is

    min( escapeX, escapeY, escapeZ )

and a `min` carries no weight at all. It gives an axis whose direction component
is ZERO the same authority as the axis the cone actually travels down - and on
every axis-aligned surface in the world (a floor, a wall, the face of a box) two
of the three components ARE zero.

Zero in exact arithmetic. In the shader they are the last bits of a round trip:
the geometric normal is interpolated in VIEW space, carried to a
camera-independent space through `invViewMatCubemap` to build the cone frame
(patch 0083), carried back, and rotated into probe space by
`toVctProbeSpaceDir`. Every one of those rotations is built from the view
matrix, and the view matrix is a function of where the camera stands IN THE
WORLD - the camera's position and, through the differencing of two absolute
world points, its look direction. So the sign of a quantity that is identically
zero is decided by the camera's distance from the world origin, and the `min`
turns that decision into a different picture.

MEASURED (lane CLIFF-32-1, 2026-09-21; a 4 m cube on the default ground at Epic,
the irradiance field off so the cones are what the picture reads, both arms in
one process, the dither off, 960x540 on a 1920x1080 rig):

  * `isNegative.y` for the first diffuse cone reads 0.5 on 100 % of the cube
    face's pixels with the scene at the origin, and flips on 50.5 % of them
    (11,321 of 22,400 sampled) with the scene 60 m out;
  * the colour composite does not notice: the cone's `alpha` is IDENTICAL
    between the two arms to 1/255 over those 22,400 pixels, and so are the
    march's step count, its lod, its `travelled`, the cone's start position in
    every cascade, and every voxel of every volume it samples;
  * the escape weight moves by 26/255 - and the escape weight is exactly what
    the sky's ambient rides (`light.w`, computeVctProbe), which is why the
    carrier is the SKY (with a black sky the whole difference is 0.004 of a
    code, measured by LATTICE-1);
  * the face: 8.82/255 (8x8-smoothed mean over the face) at 60 m, 7.59 at 120 m.

  With this patch, the same fixture in the same process:

      translation   before        after
      30 m          0.746/255     0.105/255   (max 1)
      60 m          8.823         0.109       (max 1)
      120 m         7.589         0.104       (max 1)

  i.e. the face's dependence on where the world origin is falls to the
  protocol's own floor (nine pixels of a 108,675-pixel face move by more than
  3/255 at 30 and 120 m, all of them on the cube's silhouette corner, where a
  sub-pixel rasterisation difference from the camera's own float rounding lives;
  none at 60 m).

THE FIX IS TO STOP ASKING THE CONE. The two halves of one axis texture are two
measurements of the SAME cells, composited in opposite directions, and their
mean is the direction-free reading `min3` was always after: patch 0021 derived
it as "the cell's mean occupancy up to the compositing", and an occupancy is a
property of the cell. The mean keeps every property the estimate was chosen for
- each half is still a front-to-back COMPOSITE, so a wall one voxel thick still
reports 1 along its own normal at every mip and the sealed-room leak that the
main volume's mean-occupancy alpha had (0021's own measurement: the Mirror Room
and the Showroom +46 and +51 of 255) stays shut - and it is continuous in the
cone's direction, so no last bit of a matrix can move a picture again.

THE PRICE, STATED. Six alpha fetches per anisotropic step instead of three, on
the diffuse cones and on the specular one (the two marches share this loop; the
specular cone's `escapeAlpha` is carried but not read downstream - `specAlpha`
rides `alpha` - so those three extra fetches buy nothing and are a follow-up
worth taking, not a change to make inside a fix). MEASURED, and the honest
answer is that it is below the instrument: Showroom 2 at Epic, one fixed pose,
four runs of 600 scripted frames per process with the GPU clocks locked
(2100/2550) on an idle box, read 11.45-11.61 ms/frame in one patched process,
10.92-11.35 in a second patched process and 10.98-11.03 in the base process -
the process-to-process drift (0.4 ms) is larger than any difference between the
arms. `perf.epic_steady_state`, `perf.drag_mirror_room` and `perf.hide_soak`,
which are the tree's own cost bars, all pass with the patch in.

AND THE PICTURE MOVES, BY DESIGN. The escape estimate is a different number
everywhere, so every lit surface in every scene changes a little: both
`--engine-selftest` hashes move. The A/B is in the lane's report.

WHAT IT DOES NOT FIX. The round trip itself. A cone direction that ought to be
exactly axis-aligned still arrives with a microradian of noise whose bias is a
function of the camera's world position, because it is carried through the view
matrix twice (world -> view in the vertex shader, view -> cubemap -> view in
this piece). Nothing downstream may be allowed to amplify that, which is the
rule this patch enforces; taking the round trip out - deriving the cone frame in
the camera-independent space and going straight to probe space, both of which
are world-axis-aligned - is a separate, structural change with its own pictures.

